WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.030
- [Narrator] This video
was posted this week

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.880
on Wall Street Bets, a
Reddit forum dedicated

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.670
to discussing stock and options trading.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.620
This little rocket represents GME,

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.303
the stock ticker for video
game retailer GameStop.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.100
For weeks, users of the subreddit

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.560
have been encouraging each
other to scoop up shares

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.480
and make bullish wagers on the stock.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.950
In less than a month, GME
rose from under $20 a share

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.570
to well over $300 on some days

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.190
before falling on Thursday

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.683
after brokers placed
restrictions on trading.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.250
- Wall Street has been totally stunned

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.930
by what's happened with GameStop.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.380
This has basically never happened before.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.490
- [Narrator] Alexander
Osipovich covers futures

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.630
and stock exchanges for
The Wall Street Journal

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.290
and has explored the forces behind

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.900
GameStop's dramatic swings.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.560
- GameStop has become a
battleground between small investors

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.690
who are banding together on
online forums like Reddit

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.480
and professional investors
who are shorting the stock,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.140
or betting that it will fall.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.133
- [Narrator] Here's what's
going on with GameStop.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:00:59.960
- Wall Street Bets

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.410
is one of the most
popular forums on Reddit.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.950
It's a place where people get
together, share trading ideas,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.570
and go after particular stocks

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.680
in a kind of coordinated fashion.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.580
They can cause a big run
up in that stock's price.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.800
- [Narrator] In recent weeks,
investors on Wall Street Bets

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.260
have targeted GameStop's short sellers

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.360
who saw the company as overpriced

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.500
and were betting the shares would fall.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.780
The Wall Street Bets
crowd was buying up shares

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.210
and betting the opposite,

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.400
causing the stock to go on a wild ride.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.710
- Behind that is a couple of things.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.750
One of them is momentum trading.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.280
That's when people chase gains.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.360
They see a stock is going
up, they buy more of it

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.430
because it'll likely keep going up.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.140
- [Narrator] But while the
rise might have appeared

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.840
to be as simple as average
investors buying shares

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.630
and pumping up the price, it
also had to do with options.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.730
Options are contracts that allow investors

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.250
to buy or sell a stock
at a specified price

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.440
either on or until a specified date.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.630
- It has gotten a lot easier and cheaper

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.457
for people to trade options
over the past few years.

00:01:59.457 --> 00:02:00.290
- [Commercial Announcer] Now the world

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:01.840
is your trading floor.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.330
- Commissions have come down

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.080
and brokers have invested
a lot of time and effort

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.600
into making easy to use interfaces

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.280
to allow people to buy and sell them,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.340
and even do complex strategies.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.000
- [Narrator] Options trading
values set a record last year,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.220
averaging just under 30
million contracts a day.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.730
This year, that number is over 40 million.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.790
Options come in two forms.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.480
Put options confer the
right to sell shares

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.810
under specified terms

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.140
and call options confer
the right to buy them.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.840
Many of the Wall Street Bets users

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.310
were buying call options to
bet that the price would rise.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.930
Look at the market on Tuesday,
January 26th, for example.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.270
GameStop closed at just under $148.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.103
At that moment,

00:02:44.103 --> 00:02:47.950
call options to buy GameStop
shares at $200 apiece by Friday

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.880
were trading at just $19 a share.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.260
If GameStop rallied,

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.270
the price of the options might have risen

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.050
and call holders would've been
able to sell these options

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:58.810
for a quick profit.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.620
The question is, why
were the shares surging

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.170
when much of the action was in options?

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.850
It's because when an
investor buys a call option,

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.310
someone else has to sell it to them,

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.340
typically an electronic trading firm

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.510
that buys and sells stocks.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.860
When they sell the
investor the call option,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.520
they often hedge their bet

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.900
by purchasing shares of
the stock in question.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.300
GameStop options trading volumes exploded

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.580
during the past two weeks,
especially for call options,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.160
which drove brokers to
buy more of the stock,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.230
driving it up even further.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.690
This dynamic isn't just
playing out with GameStop.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.470
Traders at Wall Street
Bets moved on to AMC,

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.000
BlackBerry and Nokia as well.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.140
Amid soaring shares,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.220
on January 28th popular online brokerages,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.200
including Robinhood and
Interactive Brokers Group,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.140
restricted access to
trading high flying stocks,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.560
including GameStop.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.370
Shares plunged.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.840
- Trading a stock like GameStop

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.180
in a situation like
this is extremely risky.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.700
If you are do playing with this stuff,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.310
you are playing with fire.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.120
It's unclear if the authorities

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.720
have any means of reining this in.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.800
- [Narrator] Wall Street
Bets users called foul,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.330
some pledging to find other ways

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.780
to participate in the market,

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.833
regardless of the chaos it causes.

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:13.416
(gentle music)

